+		<Tag>TXT_KEY_UNIT_MAYA_QUICK_WORKER_PEDIA</Tag>
+		<English>[TAB]Ancient Maya communities intensively reshaped their landscapes through agriculture and public works. At sites such as Tikal, farmers used terraces, irrigation, household gardens, arboriculture, and systems for retaining and redistributing water. Across the Maya lowlands, reservoirs, canals, wetland fields, causeways, and other infrastructure supported large populations in environments with strong seasonal constraints.[PARAGRAPH:1]These systems depended on organized human labor and continued maintenance. Rural farmers could contribute labor to construction projects and public infrastructure, while roads, waterworks, and agricultural landscapes required repeated investment to connect settlements, expand cultivable land, manage water, and sustain urban centers over long periods.</English>
