-- **Weak/late civ-specific** units **replaced by earlier/impactful ones** (e.g., Netherlands East Indiaman → Netherlands Swift Worker; German Panzer → Hussar; Mongol Keshik → Khishigten — better window with the Ger, longer medieval span, higher hammer rate, and more historical; English **Redcoat** → **Yeoman archer** — crossbow-based, fits England’s defensive profile, and gives cover when short on early bonuses; Holy Roman Landsknecht → Houfnice — cannon-based aligns with HRE’s aggressive profile, hits the tight medieval window, and avoids Landsknecht’s obsolescence/balance issues (anti-melee niche fades; weak vs. Cuirassiers)). Detailed civ-specific unit documentation now lives in [Assets rebalancing: Civ-specific units rework](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Assets_Rebalancing.md#civ-specific-units-rework). For updated in-game data, see also Sevopedia Unit.
+- **Weak/late or overly narrow civ-specific** units are often **replaced by earlier, broader or more impactful ones** (e.g., Netherlands East Indiaman → Netherlands Swift Worker; German Panzer → Hussar; Mongol Keshik → Khishigten — better window with the Ger, longer medieval span, higher hammer rate, and more historical; English **Redcoat** → **Yeoman archer** — crossbow-based, fits England’s defensive profile, and gives cover when short on early bonuses; Holy Roman Landsknecht → Houfnice — cannon-based aligns with HRE’s aggressive profile, hits the tight medieval window, and avoids Landsknecht’s obsolescence/balance issues (anti-melee niche fades; weak vs. Cuirassiers); (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6121+) Maya Holkan → Quick Worker, replacing a narrow early Spearman role with a broadly useful +25% work-rate civilian UU). Detailed civ-specific unit documentation now lives in [Assets rebalancing: Civ-specific units rework](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Assets_Rebalancing.md#civ-specific-units-rework). For updated in-game data, see also Sevopedia Unit.
+- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6121+) **No naval civ-specific units as of now**: naval replacements can be effectively useless on land-heavy maps but disproportionately valuable on naval-heavy maps, especially when most other civilizations have no comparable naval replacement. Current examples of moving away from that design include the **Netherlands East Indiaman → Swift Worker** and **Portugal Carrack → Cacador** reworks. This keeps civ-specific unit value relevant across map types without requiring a much larger roster of naval counterparts; ordinary naval units and naval warfare remain available as usual. See [Assets rebalancing: Civ-specific units rework](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Assets_Rebalancing.md#civ-specific-units-rework) for the fuller rationale.
 - **Broad unit reworks/rebalances:** e.g., French **Musketeer** — Move 2 → 1; gains anti-mounted and small collateral kit for a stronger Renaissance+ role (especially vs Cuirassier units and their upgrades).
 - **Bonus requirements relaxed (selective):** Some units now accept additional **bonus** prerequisites where appropriate (to avoid odd lockouts). Examples: **Samurai** can use **Copper** as well as Iron; **Swordsman-class civ-specific variants** (e.g., **Roman Legionary**, **Incan Quechua**) can also use **Copper** (only the **Gallic Warrior** could before; which seemed inconsistent and unbalanced without a clear reason why: now equalized); **Crossbowman-class civ-specific variants** (e.g., **Zhuge Nu**, **Disciplined Crossbowman**) Bronze/copper hardware was common (Benin bronzes; Chinese bronze/iron mix long before ubiquitous steel) and to buff them; Holy Roman **Houfnice** can also use **Copper** (historically plausible bronze artillery). The **generic Swordsman** and the **generic Cannon** remain **Iron-only** to preserve strategic pressure. Thematically consistent. See Sevopedia/XML.
 - Units more **versatile:** e.g., Swords defend better; Archers can attack more credibly.

